#    improves temporal locality for rows of A that participate together.
kernel_lu = divide_loop(kernel_lu, "i", TILE_I, ["io", "ii"], tail="guard")

# 2) In the upper-triangular update, reorder the (j, k) loops so that we
#    iterate over k first and j innermost:
#
#       Original:
#           for j in seq(i, n):
#               for k in seq(0, i):
#                   A[i, j] += -A[i, k] * A[k, j]
#
#       After reordering:
#           for k in seq(0, i):
#               for j in seq(i, n):
#                   A[i, j] += -A[i, k] * A[k, j]
#
#    This reordering preserves the mathematical result (the body is a
#    reduction into A[i, j]) but gives better row-major memory access for
#    A[k, j] and A[i, j].
kernel_lu = reorder_loops(kernel_lu, "j #1")

# 3) After the above reordering, the second j-loop (the one inside the
#    upper-triangular update) is innermost. Tile that j-loop to improve
#    cache reuse along the row dimension.
kernel_lu = divide_loop(kernel_lu, "j #1", TILE_J, ["jo", "ji"], tail="guard")

# 4) Optionally, perform algebraic simplifications and dead-code elimination
#    on the scheduled kernel.
kernel_lu = simplify(kernel_lu)
